     1	//! Termio is responsible for "terminal IO." Specifically, this is the
     2	//! reading and writing of bytes for the underlying pty or pty-like device.
     3	//!
     4	//! Termio is constructed of a few components:
     5	//!   - Termio - The main shared struct that has common logic across all
     6	//!     backends and mailboxes (defined below).
     7	//!   - Backend - Responsible for the actual physical IO. For example, one
     8	//!     implementation creates a subprocess, allocates and assigns a pty,
     9	//!     and sets up a read thread on the pty.
    10	//!   - Mailbox - Responsible for storing/dispensing event messages to
    11	//!     the backend. This exists separately from backends because termio
    12	//!     is built to be both single and multi-threaded.
    13	//!
    14	//! Termio supports (and recommends) multi-threaded operation. Multi-threading
    15	//! enables the read/writes to generally happen on separate threads and
    16	//! almost always improves throughput and latency under heavy IO load. To
    17	//! enable threading, use the Thread struct. This wraps a Termio, requires
    18	//! specific backend/mailbox capabilities, and sets up the necessary threads.
